Vendored crates:
    collections, gpui, gpui_macros, http_client, media, refineable,
    refineable/derive_refineable, sum_tree, util, util_macros, tooling/perf

Modifications made to the vendored sources (per Apache-2.0 section 4(b)):

  * crates/sum_tree:
      - Removed the `ztracing` (AGPL-3.0) dependency. The `#[instrument]`
        attributes it provided were repointed to the permissive `tracing`
        crate, which sum_tree already depended on.
      - Removed the `zlog` (GPL-3.0) test-only dev-dependency. Its single use
        (`zlog::init_test()` in a `#[cfg(test)]` block) was replaced with a
        no-op. No GPL/AGPL code remains in the vendored tree.

  * crates/gpui:
      - Removed the `reqwest_client` dev-dependency (a non-vendored Zed crate).
        gpui's test suite is not built when gpui is consumed as a path
        dependency, so this has no effect on the library build.
      - geometry.rs: expressed the const golden-ratio fraction as consecutive
        Fibonacci terms, preserving its exact f32 value and avoiding a newer
        standard-library constant requirement. Added application integration
        checks for the float value and pixel/rem geometry.
      - window.rs/platform.rs: added a test-support-only accessor to retain
        the exact input handler installed by paint for native protocol tests.
        Added an opaque input-context token that changes synchronously on
        true focus, blur and activation transitions, including round trips
        before repaint. No-op focus and redraw retain the token.
      - window.rs: added a test-support-only mouse-down dispatch helper to
        exercise painted callbacks before deferred focus notifications run.

No other source modifications were made; the vendored crate sources are
otherwise unchanged from upstream.

The X11 input-method client is patched locally under `vendor/xim/`.
    zed-xim 0.4.0-zed, xim-parser 0.2.1, xim-ctext 0.3.0, xim-gen 0.1.0
    Copyright (c) 2020 Riey; Zed contributors
    Source: https://github.com/zed-industries/xim-rs
    Revision: 16f35a2c881b815a2b6cdfd6687988e84f8447d8
    License: MIT; original LICENSE files retained beside each crate.
    Modifications: return the existing InvalidReply error for malformed
    Reset/Commit/Preedit compound text; propagate reset-handler errors;
    reject Chinese/Korean compound-text bytes that would overflow or wrap.
    Consume the ASCII GL designation ESC(B without inserting it into text,
    preserve initial literal prefixes, and reject invalid escape sequences.
    Refuse high GR bytes following this reset under a prior non-Latin GR
    designation; independent GL/GR decoding remains incomplete.
    Bound HotKeyTriggers counts before allocation, assign parsed states to
    their matching keys, and include all fields in its serialized size.
    Keep that fix in the generator snippet as well as generated parser.
    Preserve XIM synchronous flags for character and keysym commits;
    forward combined keysym/text commits through the text callback, and
    acknowledge successful synchronous commits after callback processing.
    Reject unsupported X transport versions before connecting, validate
    handshake format, and bound single-ClientMessage payloads. Unsupported
    transport sends return a typed error instead of panicking.
    X11 client transport 0.1 now sends ordered multi-ClientMessage packets
    and reassembles declared packets within a 262,144-byte bound, with
    fallible allocation and final-padding validation. Transport 0.2 and
    PropertyNotify modes remain unsupported. No native composition routing
    or generic server transport contract changed.
    Client transport 0.0 property input validates announced packet lengths
    before bounded GetProperty requests, preserves checked offsets across
    APPEND notifications, and bounds pending atoms/backlog. Reply metadata,
    header length and decoded request consumption are checked before delivery;
    related errors retire only their property's pending offset.
    Added actual X11 property-transport/parser tests with an in-memory
    connection, and compound-text decoding regressions. The optional
    parser generator is retained to preserve the original manifests.

